Raw MRI data is increasingly utilized in studies, yet generalizing quality control (QC) protocols from large training sets to new subjects remains difficult due to inherent biases. This paper evaluates a raw-data QC pipeline on the recently released ABCD study (N=10,882), assessing indices that range from basic image metrics to advanced morphological measures of brain tissue. We compare a baseline pipeline with an enhanced version designed to increase robustness and address dataset bias. Our findings indicate that while many QC indices fail to generalize (generalization error roughly 3x the training standard deviation), the indices measuring signal artifacts and head alignment demonstrated the best generalization (approx. 1/6th of training error). Furthermore, the baseline pipeline suffered from generalization bias, which was reduced by a factor of six in the enhanced pipeline. These results underscore the impact of low-level artifacts and head alignment issues—factors often overlooked in previous QC frameworks—on the interpretability of higher-level phenotypes derived from raw-data QC in large-scale studies. The code is available at https://github.com/haritzf/MRIRawQC.

